entry.summary:
When you write complex CSS rules, you often have to handle style conflicts. The concepts of cascade and specificity significantly affect the final CSS, so sometimes it becomes difficult to understand and maintain it. CSS nesting seems like a better alternative to create visually сlear code, and along with @scope it offers completely new possibilities for grouping and isolating CSS rules. Such power is often taken too literally, which leads to some misconceptions about @scope. Let's dispel common myths and take a pragmatic look at the @scope real power.
@scope fully isolates styles


No, it doesn't. CSS property inheritance is not prevented. Properties such as font-size, font-weight, color can use the computed value of the parent element - in other words they can cross the @scope limit. If …
